From 2c17d0fcb7746d8a46fd70bdd02c545ee2a907bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stefane Fermigier <sf@abilian.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 21:24:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 26/82] OdfFile writer: no text:span inside text-only fields

A DOCX field result is a sequence of runs, and each run opened a
text:span inside the field element: <text:page-number><text:span>5
</text:span></text:page-number>. Every element start_field makes holds
text only, so the schema rejects it. start_span now declines inside a
field level; end_span already pops only a span, so the pairing holds.

The cost: the formatting of the result runs, a bold page number say, is
not kept. Valid ODF can carry it only on a span around the field, and
a complex field is opened before its first run's span, so keeping it
means reordering start_run, which this does not attempt.

Found in 86 of LibreOffice's 3794 ODF test documents after a round trip.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 OdfFile/Writer/Format/odf_text_context.cpp | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/OdfFile/Writer/Format/odf_text_context.cpp b/OdfFile/Writer/Format/odf_text_context.cpp
index 0919c7124a..3c07be421f 100644
--- a/OdfFile/Writer/Format/odf_text_context.cpp
+++ b/OdfFile/Writer/Format/odf_text_context.cpp
@@ -385,6 +385,10 @@ int odf_text_context::get_last_level()
 void odf_text_context::start_span(bool styled)
 {
 	if (styles_context_ == NULL || single_paragraph_)return;
+	// Every element start_field makes (text:page-number, text:date, text:bookmark-ref, ...)
+	// holds text only, and the runs of a field result would otherwise each become a span
+	// inside it. Skipping the span is safe: end_span pops only a span.
+	if (false == current_level_.empty() && current_level_.back().type == 3) return;
 
 	office_element_ptr span_elm;
 	create_element(L"text", L"span", span_elm, odf_context_);
